## 1. Introduction
The ever-increasing serious water pollution caused by rapid industrialization has attracted tremendous attention among the scientific community [1,2]. Especially, all sorts of refractory organic compounds pose potential threats to aquatic organisms and human health [3,4]. Conventional sewage treatment processes cannot effectively remove these organic compounds to meet increasingly stringent water quality standards [5–7]. Nowadays, advanced oxidation processes (AOPs) based on $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ activation display promising prospect against removal of various refractory organic compounds pollutants, and is widely utilized due to its green byproducts of $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and $0_{2}$ [8,9]. Fenton oxidation process is particularly preferable for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ activation benefited from its mild reaction conditions, simple operation procedure and strong oxidative capacity [10,11]. However, the traditional homogeneous Fenton suffers from the narrow pH applicable range, slow Fe(II) regeneration and undesirable iron sludge generation [12,13]. In this regard, heterogeneous photo-Fenton is considered to be the most promising Fenton-derived technology because it can avoid the above-mentioned shortcomings of homogeneous Fenton [14]. Significantly, since the photogenerated electrons participate in the Fenton process, making it a green and sustainable catalytic process [15].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), a class of emerging porous materials self-assembled from inorganic metal clusters and organic bridging ligands, have been utilized in various fields like environmental purification [16,17] and catalysis [18–20]. Iron-based metal organic frameworks (Fe-MOFs) have versatile intriguing traits as typical photo-Fenton catalysts, such as large specific surface area (SSA), well-defined metal nodes, good visible light responsiveness, tailored functionality, enriched functionality, etc. [21]. Most importantly, the well-dispersed metal coordinately unsaturated sites (M CUSs) in the Fe-MOFs framework, as Lewis acid sites (LAS), tend to coordinate with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Lewis base) [22,23], which makes Fe-MOFs ideal catalysts for the heterogeneous photoFenton process. Unfortunately, a majority of Fe centers are fully bound by organic ligands resulting in inadequate Fe CUSs [24]. Besides, the local electron density at Fe CUSs is closely associated with the reactivity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ activation, since $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ as the electron acceptor is reduced to ∙OH by electrons around Fe CUSs, which needs amount of electrons to participate in the reaction [14]. However, the symmetrical electronic distribution of Fe-O clusters [25] and the fast electron-hole recombination caused the random charge transfer, collectively result in a lower local electron density at Fe CUSs [26]. To this end, regulating Lewis acidity and local electron density of Fe sites are two pivotal elements for improving the photo-Fenton catalytic performance of Fe-MOFs. In addition, amelioration of pore structure, for instance, increasing pore sizes from micropore to mesopore is conducive to highly-efficient utilization of ROS.
Owing to its flexible and tunable structure, a second metal can be easily doped into the original metal node structure of Fe-oxo clusters, and thus the local coordination structure and neighboring chemical environment of metal centers are changed accordingly. Previous studies suggest that the ligand missing defects can be formed as the result of the difference in electronegativity and coordination number between Fe and doped hetero-metal ions [27]. Also, electron redistribution would occur after hetero-metal ions doping, which may increase the local electron density and improve the photo-Fenton catalytic performance of Fe-MOFs [28]. Inspired by this, we speculated that the stronger Lewis acidity of Fe-MOFs and more electron aggregation on Fe CUSs may be obtained through effective modification of metal doping.
Among the recent reported Fe-MOFs, MIL-88B with chemical formula $\mathrm{Fe_{3}(\mu_{3}{-}O)(B D C)_{3}(H_{2}O)_{2}X}$ (BDC: 1,4-benzenedicarboxylicacid; X: compensating anion), is of particular interest in virtue of its relatively simple synthetic procedure and good water stability [24]. Ce ions possess excellent properties of multivalence, flexible coordination, and low electronegativity relative to Fe ions. Based on these properties, the introduction of Ce may effectively regulate the properties of the resulting MIL-88B. In this work, MIL-88B were exploited as a platform to construct novel bimetal organic frameworks by Ce doping. Tetracycline (TC), one of the extensively used antibiotics, was chosen as a model pollutant owning to its persistence and ecotoxicity in aquatic environment [26]. The effect of Ce doping on the metal node structure of MIL-88B and its photo-Fenton activity, viz., structure–activity relationship was examined, respectively. Specifically, the intensity of Lewis acidity, pore structure and local electron distribution at Fe CUSs before and after Ce doping were compared based on advanced characterization technologies and DFT theoretical calculation. Moreover, the influence of operational parameters including catalyst dosage, initial pH and $\mathrm{H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ concentration on the TC degradation was systematically investigated. Finally, the TC degradation mechanism and reusability and stability of catalysts were also investigated.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Catalyst synthesis
MIL-88B(Fe) was fabricated according to previously reported protocols with slight modifications (see Text S1 in SI). For $\mathsf{F e}_{1}.$ - $\mathbf{\nabla_{x}C e_{x}}$ -MIL-88B samples with different $\times$ $\mathbf{\dot{X}}=0.1$ , 0.2, or 0.3), the same scenario was employed except for the different molar ratios (1:9, 2:8 and 3:7) of $\mathsf{C e}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ and $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ . The fabrication process of $\mathrm{Fe_{1-x}C e_{x}–M I L-88B}$ is schematically shown in Fig. 1.
## 2.2. Characterizations
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were obtained on Hitachi S-4800 microscopy, equipped with energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) accessory. X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were measured on Bruker D8 Advance diffractometer with $\mathtt{C u}\mathtt{K}\mathtt{\simeq}$ ( $\?=$ $0.15418~\mathrm{nm}$ ) anode. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) images were obtained on a JEM2100F Field Emission Electron Microscope (JEOL, Japan). Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra were conducted at Bruker Tensor 27 spectrometer. X-ray photo-electron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra were recorded on Thermo Scientific K-Alpha instrument. Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area and pore size distribution were determined from $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms tested by Micromeritics ASAP 2020HD88. Electron spin resonance (ESR) spectra were recorded upon Bruker EMXplus spectrometer to investigate the ligand vacancies. Pyridine-chemisorbed FTIR (Py-FTIR) spectra were recorded using PerkinElmer-FTIR Spectrometer-Frontier. Diffuse reflectance spectroscopy (DRS) was examined by Shimadzu UV-3600 spectrophotometer. Transient photocurrent curves (TPC) and Electrochemical Impedance Spectroscopy Nyquist (EIS) were detected by an electrochemical workstation connected to Chenhua CHI660E software. The details of the DFT calculation were described in the Supporting Information.
## 2.3. Experiments and analyses
All experiments were carried out with magnetic stirring at room temperature and repeated independently for three times. For batch-mode adsorption experiments, $_{0.01\mathrm{~g~}}$ material and $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ TC solution ${\mathrm{\Gamma}}50{\mathrm{~mg}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{L}}^{-1}.$ ) were mixed and stirred for $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ in the dark, and $3~\mathrm{mL}$ aliquot was withdrawn at a certain interval time through $0.22\upmu\mathrm{m}$ syringe filter. The TC concentration was analyzed by UV–vis PERSEE TU-1900 spectrophotometer at $357\ \mathrm{nm}$ . For photo-Fenton experiments, a certain amount of catalyst was mixed with $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ TC solution $(50\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}),$ for $60\mathrm{{min}}$ in the dark to reach adsorption–desorption equilibrium. The different initial pH value was adjusted using $0.1\mathrm{~M~}\mathrm{NaOH}$ or 0.1 M HCl. Then, a certain dosage of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was added into above system and photo-Fenton degradation process was initiated by a 300 W Xenon lamp $(\lambda>420\mathrm{nm})$ . The circulating water was used to maintain the temperature constant during the process. $3~\mathrm{mL}$ sample was taken out by a syringe at regular time intervals and added excessive catalase to quench excess $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ for further analysis. The recovered catalyst was washed several times with ethanol, and dried for the next batch process. The mineralization rate of TC was detected on the TOC-VCPH analyzer. The concentration of dissolved iron was also monitored by UV–vis PERSEE TU-1900 spectrophotometer at $510\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The quenching experiments for OH, $\cdot0_{2}^{-}$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ were carried out with $t$ -Butyl alcohol (TBA), benzoquinone (BQ) and methanol (MeOH), respectively. Electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectra were implemented on Bruker EMXplus- $_{.6/1}$ to monitor the existence of OH.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Material characterizations
SEM images clearly displayed the apparent morphology of the MIL-88B and Fe1- $\mathbf{\nabla.}_{\mathbf{x}}\mathbf{Ce_{x}}.$ -MIL-88B. MIL-88B exhibits spindle rods with an average length of ca. $440~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and width of ca. $93\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 2a). With Ce doping proportion increasing from 0 to 0.2, the appearance of spindle rods became longer and narrower (Fig. 2b-c), the average length grew to ca. $784~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ and the average width decreased to ca. $57\mathrm{nm}$ with ${\bf{X}}=0.2$ . However, with further increasing Ce doping amount $\mathrm{(x}=0.3\mathrm{)}$ ), the length and width of $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.7}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.3^{-}}$ MIL-88B have the opposite variation tendency (Fig. 2d), becoming shorter and wider in contrast with that of $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B. Evidently, the crystal growth behavior of the MIL-88B was altered in the presence of the Ce. Then, the EDS was employed to investigate the elemental distribution of $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B. As shown in Fig. S1, EDS images clearly demonstrated the uniform distributions of Fe and Ce elements throughout the architecture, further proving the successful incorporation with Ce into the frameworks.
The crystallographic structure information of MIL-88B and $\mathrm{Fe_{1-x}C e_{x}–M I L-88B}$ were determined by XRD (Fig. 2e). The XRD pattern of MIL-88B agrees well with that of simulated one as reported previously [29,30]. $\mathrm{Fe_{1-x}C e_{x}–M I L-88B}$ and MIL-88B exhibit semblable diffraction pattern, suggesting that these bimetallic MOFs are isostructural to MIL-88B. Interestingly, the intensity ratio of (002) plane to (101) plane gradually increased, presenting a tendency for crystallographic orientation. Noteworthily, no signal of cerium oxide or hydroxide was observed in their XRD patterns, but a weak yet discernible peak appeared at 2h of $12.6^{\circ}$ , indicative of the ordered special-cluster nanoregions [31–33]. Thus, Ce incorporation via isostructural replacement of Fe probably occurred and formed new Fe-O-Ce clusters in the framework nodes of $\mathsf{F e}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{C e}_{\mathrm{x}^{-}}$ MIL-88B [31,34]. What’s more, the peak of (002) plane gradually became wider and split into two peaks with further increasing Ce amount, demonstrating the distortion/destroy in long-range order of MIL-88B [31,35,36]. The localized crystallographic structures of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}88\mathrm{B}$ was further disclosed using the TEM and HRTEM images (Fig. S2a and b). The resolved lattice fringes with a d-spacing of about $0.91\ \mathrm{nm}$ correspond to the (002) plane of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}88\mathrm{B}$ crystal. Crucially, local lattice distortions were observed, indicating that Ce doping can cause deformation of the crystal lattice and may produce a large number of structural defects.
The surface functional groups and the formation of coordinated polymers in MIL-88B and $\mathrm{Fe_{1-x}C e_{x}–M I L-88B}$ were detected by using FT-IR (Fig. 2f). No significant differences were observed among their spectra, suggesting that Ce doping rarely affected the surface functional groups. Notably, in the spectrum of MIL-88B, the band at $550\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is attributed to the bridging coordination between Fe and carboxylates groups $(0{-}\mathsf{C}{=}0)$ of BDC [37]. In contract, as for $\mathrm{Fe}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ $\mathsf{C e}_{\mathbf{x}}$ -MIL-88B, the bands slightly shifted to $557~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . It has been reported that the vibration peak of Ce-O is usually located at $560~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which proved the successful incorporation of Ce in the Fe-O clusters [38–40]. These observations are in good accordance with the above XRD results.
XPS was carried out on MIL-88B and $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}\mathrm{-MIL-}88\mathrm{B}$ to further compare their electronic structure. As depicted in Fig. 2g, Fe $2{\tt p}$ spectrum of MIL-88B presents two sets of fitted peaks assigned to Fe $2{\mathsf{p}}_{1/2}$ and Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ , with the binding energy (BE) of 726.4 and $712.9\mathrm{eV}$ (corresponding to Fe of $\mu_{3}$ -OFe), 724.9 and $711.4\mathrm{eV}$ (corresponding to Fe of FeAOAC (BDC)), along with a satellite peak at
$717.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ [24,41], that confirmed the valence state of Fe as $+\ [\mathrm{III}]$ . In comparison, the BE of Fe 2p in the $\mathsf{F e}_{0.8}\mathsf{C e}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B slightly shifted to lower energy. In particular, the BE of fitted peak of Fe ascribing to $_{\mathrm{Fe-O-C}}$ (BDC) was decreased by $0.4~\mathrm{eV}$ due to the smaller electronegativity of Ce than Fe leading to the asymmetry of the electronic distribution. Therefore, Fe-O-Ce clusters were formed and the local electron density at Fe center was increased [25,42]. As can be seen from Fig. S3a, O 1 s spectrum of MIL-88B gives two deconvoluted peaks located at 533.4 and $531.8\mathrm{eV}$ , which are assigned to surface oxygen (adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ) and lattice oxygen $\cdot\mu_{3}–0$ of FeAOAC (BDC)), respectively [12]. Compared to that of MIL-88B, the BE of lattice oxygen in $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B was negatively shifted by $0.5~\mathrm{eV}$ , indicating a partial missing of the lattice oxygen. That further corroborated the formation of crystal defects. Additionally, Ce 3d spectrum of $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B shows the multiple splitting peaks corresponding to the ${\mathsf{C e}}({\mathrm{III}})$ state. (Fig. S3b). In conclusion, the above results jointly evidenced the successful synthesis of $\mathsf{F e}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{C e}_{\mathrm{x}}$ -MIL-88B with the formation of Fe-O-Ce clusters.
## 3.2. Evaluation of photo-Fenton performance
Photo-Fenton process includes surface adsorption and catalytic oxidation degradation. As depicted in Fig. 3a, the adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{Fe_{1-x}C e_{x}–M I L-88B}$ towards TC was remarkably enhanced compared with MIL-88B, and followed the sequence of $\mathsf{F e}_{0.8}\mathsf{C e}_{0.2^{-}}$ $\mathrm{MIL{-}88{\cal B}>{\cal F}e_{0.7}C e_{0.3}-M I L-88{\cal B}>{\cal F}e_{0.9}C e_{0.1}-M I L-88>M I L-88{\cal B}.}$ After adsorption–desorption equilibrium, photo-Fenton degradation experiments of TC over MIL-88B and $\mathrm{Fe_{1-x}C e_{x}–M I L-88B}$ were conducted (Fig. 3a) and the corresponding data were fitted to pseudo-first-order model (Fig. 3b). $\mathrm{Fe}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Ce}_{\mathrm{x}}{-}\mathrm{MIL}{-}88\mathrm{B}$ gave higher TC degradation rates than that of MIL-88B. The variation trend of TC degradation rates over different catalysts in the photo-Fenton degradation process was in line with their adsorption capacity. Among them, $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}\mathrm{-MIL-}88\mathrm{B}$ achieved the highest catalytic activity, i.e., the maximal degradation efficiency of TC $(87.9\ \%)$ within $15{\mathrm{~min}}$ with a rate constant reaching up to $0.110\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ Compared with other recently reported Fenton-like catalysts (Tab. S1), $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B still exhibits excellent catalytic performance, further indicating it is a desirable and alternative candidate in the application of heterogeneous photo-Fenton for environment remediation.
In order to reveal the main reasons for the improved photoFenton catalytic performance of MIL-88B after Ce doping, the textual properties, the amount of M CUSs, the local electron density of Fe sites and the opto-electrical properties were taken into consideration, respectively. First, $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms were acquired to probe the textural properties of materials. As depicted in Fig. 4a, all isotherms followed the typical type IV isotherm of IUPAC (International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry) classification with clear $\mathrm{H}_{3}$ hysteresis loop, reflecting the feature of mesoporous structure [43]. Fig. 4b shows the pore size distribution of the samples, from which it could be noted that MIL-88B possessed the pronounced peak at $4.00\ \mathrm{nm}$ while $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.9^{-}}$ $\mathsf{C e}_{0.1}{\mathsf{-M I L}}{\mathsf{-}}88\mathsf{B}$ had the major peak at $10.00\ \mathrm{nm}$ and a wide peak at around $30.00{-}50.00\ \mathrm{nm}$ . For $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B, the peak at $10.00\ \mathrm{nm}$ disappeared, and the intensity of the wide peak at around $30.00{-}50.00\ \mathrm{nm}$ was further significantly strengthened. The evolution trend of the pore size distribution above indicated that Ce doping could enlarge the pore size of MIL-88B. Meanwhile, as the Ce doping ratio increased from 0.00 to 0.20, the specific BET surface areas suffered a diminishing progression (from big to small) (Tab. S2). That could be explained by ligand missing defects. As shown in Fig. 4c, with the loss of one ligand, two adjacent pores combined into one bigger pore, resulting in the enlarged pore size and correspondingly smaller specific surface area [44–46]. However, compared with $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B, the pore size of $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.7}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.3^{-}}$ MIL-88B was not further enlarged, and the specific BET surface areas was not further reduced, which may be attributed to the pore collapse caused by high concentration ligand missing defects. Then, ESR was applied to further check whether more defects were formed after Ce doping. As shown in Fig. S4, the weak signal peak was occurred at $g=2.00$ for MIL-88B owing to the missing terminal $_{\mathrm{H_{2}O/O H}}$ ligands [24]. Notably, the intensity of signal peak for $\mathsf{F e}_{0.8^{-}}$ $\mathsf{C e}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B was significantly higher than that of MIL-88B, demonstrating the above hypothesis, i.e., the absence of organic linkers between two metals nodes [47], resulting in the formation of more M CUCs [35].
The enlarged mesopores could facilitate the exposure and utilization of internal adsorption sites, thus more TC molecules were adsorbed on the inner surface through hydrophobic effect and the $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction between the benzene rings of TC and $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2^{-}}$
MIL-88B, causing remarkably increased adsorption capacity as shown in Fig. 3a. The adsorption enrichment of TC molecules around M CUSs resulted in the high collision possibility between the TC molecules and OH, which favored TC degradation. On the other hand, the enlarged mesopores could be valuable for the fast TC molecule diffusion toward the pore channel, leading to the efficient mass-transfer rate. As illustrated in Fig. 5a and Tab. S3, the mass-transfer rate of TC molecules over all catalysts could be assessed by the value of $k_{2d}$ (according to Weber-Morris intraparticle diffusion model (Text. S3)), and followed the order of $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2^{-}}$ $\mathrm{MIL-88B}>\mathrm{Fe}_{0.7}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.3}\mathrm{-MIL-88B}>\mathrm{Fe}_{0.9}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.1}\mathrm{-M}$ IL- $88>$ MIL-88B. The largest $k_{2d}$ value $(0.032\ \mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}{\cdot}m i n^{-1/2}})$ was found on $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2^{-}}$ MIL-88B, which is consistent with their size of mesopores. The rapid mass-transfer made TC molecules contact with the adsorption site quickly, then the surrounding OH could be utilized to degrade TC, and the adsorption sites were further released for the next round of adsorption-degradation. Both the strong adsorption capacity and the efficient mass-transfer rate enabled $\mathsf{F e}_{0.8^{-}}$ $\mathsf{C e}_{0.2}{\mathsf{-M I L}}{\mathsf{-}}88\mathsf{B}$ to efficiently utilize OH in photo-Fenton process, which was further demonstrated by means of synchronous and asynchronous experiments. In the synchronous removal experiment, adsorption and photo-Fenton degradation proceed simultaneously while in the asynchronous one, photo-Fenton degradation starts after adsorption–desorption equilibrium. As clearly observed in Fig. 5b, TC degradation by asynchronism was 1.6 times that by synchronism within $15{\mathrm{~min}}$ , corroborating the effective utilization of OH via pre-enrichment of organic contaminants.
The M CUCs as LAS are pivotal active components for MOFs and can be characterized by $\mathsf{P y}$ -FTIR spectroscopy. Fig. 6a and b show the Py-FTIR spectra of MIL-88B and $\mathsf{F e}_{0.8}\mathsf{C e}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B at different degassing temperatures (i.e., $25~^{\circ}C$ , $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , respectively. Two peaks positioned at 1070 and $1042~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are assigned to the typical $\mathbf{v}_{18\mathrm{a}}$ and $\mathbf{v}_{12}$ vibration modes on the coordination of pyridine molecules to M CUCs (Fe or Ce) [37,48]. To quantify the amount of M CUCs, the Lewis acidity was calculated with $0.6\ \mathrm{cm}{\cdot}\upmu\mathrm{m}0^{-1}$ as the molar absorption coefficient of the peak at $1070~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [49,50]. The Lewis acidity at $25~^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ , $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , and $250^{\circ}\mathsf C$ corresponds to the numbers of total, medium and strong LAS, respectively. Obviously, $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}\mathrm{-MIL-88B}$ had a noticeably increased Lewis acidity in comparison to MIL-88B. Among them, the total and medium Lewis acidity increased by 2.08 and 2.39 times, respectively, while the strong Lewis acidity only slightly increased (Table S4). It appeared that defective $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL88B induced the generation of more medium/weak Lewis acidity, which provided sufficient active sites for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ coordination in photo-Fenton degradation process.
To gain insight into the influences of Ce doping on the electronic configurations of MIL-88B, the theoretical calculation was carried out. Fig. 6c shows the three-dimensional charge density difference plot of the MIL-88B and $\mathrm{Fe_{0.8}C e_{0.2}–M I L–88B}$ , where the wathet regions represent electron density depletion, whilst yellow one electron density gain. Apparently, the charge density of $\mathsf{F e}_{0.8}\mathsf{C e}_{0.2^{-}}$
MIL-88B was redistributed and became non-uniform compared with MIL-88B. At the same time, it could be clearly observed that $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B had the electron-rich environment at Fe CUSs with ligand missing defects (Fe-2) (Fig. 6d), which matched well with the results of XPS spectra. The above phenomenon is closely related to the electronegativity difference between Ce and Fe and the role of ligand missing defect as electron trap sites. For one, the lower electronegativity of Ce than Fe caused the asymmetric electronic distribution at Fe-O-Ce clusters, making the electron accumulation on Fe CUSs. For another, the electron trap state properties of the ligand missing defects enable directional electron transfer from oxygen to Fe CUSs, generating more available electrons.
To further investigate the contribution of local electron-rich density of Fe CUSs on $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ conversion, the activation energy barrier was calculated. It is well known that there are two different pathways of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ activation in Fenton process: the direct reduction of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ activation benefited from its electron–acceptor property of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (pathway I: $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}+\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\cdot0\mathrm{H}+0\mathrm{H}^{-};$ , and the decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into $\cdot0_{2}^{-}$ via donating one electron (pathway II: $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}+\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2^{-}}+2\mathrm{H}^{+})$ [51]. Notably, the Fenton process favors ∙OH over $\cdot0_{2}^{-}$ because the former has a higher oxidation potential. With regard to the first pathway, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ coordinated with Fe CUSs of MIL-88B via Fe-O bonds, but $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was poorly activated according to the marginally elongated $_{0-0}$ bond (lo-o) from 1.488 Å to 1.499 Å (Fig. 6e). In contrast, $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B could provide the Fe and Ce CUSs to combine with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , the substantial electron donation from electron-rich Fe CUSs and another Ce CUSs populated the O atoms of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ by forming bridged FeAOAOACe complexes, which favored for homolytic cleavage of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into ∙OH (lo-o: 2.045 Å) (Fig. 6f). Obviously, the activation energy barrier over $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B $(0.28~\mathrm{eV})$ was much lower than that of MIL-88B $(0.62\ \mathrm{eV})$ . Thus, the highly increased electrons density around Fe CUS strengthened the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ activation with lower activation energy barrier. It reveals that Fe CUSs with more electrons are ideal $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ activation electronic configuration.
The light harvesting ability of MIL-88B and $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B was explored via UV–vis DRS. As shown in Fig. S5, the light absorption intensity in the visible light region was slightly strengthened for $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B. Also, the band energy $\mathrm{(E_{g})}$ of MIL-88B and $\mathsf{F e}_{0.8}\mathsf{C e}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B was calculated by the Tauc equation and their band gap values were determined as 2.81 and $2.72\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. Thus, the improved photo-Fenton catalytic performance of $\mathsf{F e}_{0.8^{-}}$ $\mathsf{C e}_{0.2}{\mathsf{-M I L}}{\mathsf{-}}88\mathsf{B}$ cannot be attributed to the difference in their optical properties. TPC and EIS were measured to probe the separation and transport efficiency of surface charge carriers. As demonstrated in
Fig. 7a and b, $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B revealed the higher photocurrent intensity and smaller semicircle in contrast with that of MIL-88B, implying that the more efficient charge separation and transfer were occurred in $\mathsf{F e}_{0.8}\mathsf{C e}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B. It could be concluded that Ce doping is beneficial for separation and transfer of photogenerated charge carriers under visible light irradiation.
## 3.3. Mechanism of efficient TC degradation
To clarify the mechanism of TC degradation in photo-Fenton process, the removal efficiency of different systems (i.e., visible light, visible light $\boldsymbol{I}_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B/visible light, $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2^{-}}$ $\mathsf{M I L{-}}88\mathsf{B/H}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B/visible light $H_{2}O_{2}$ ) were first assessed, and the corresponding results were presented in Fig. 8a and b. Significantly, $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B/visible light $H_{2}O_{2}$ exhibited the highest TC degradation efficiency and the largest $k$ value, while in the absence of visible light, $\mathrm{Fe_{0.8}C e_{0.2}\mathrm{-MIL\mathrm{-}88B/H_{2}O_{2}}}$ obtained an inferior performance of TC degradation, demonstrating that visible light could greatly accelerate the process of Fenton oxidation by supplying photogenerated electron to participate in the multicycles of $\mathrm{Fe(II)/Fe(III)}$ and $\mathsf{C e}(\mathrm{III})/\mathsf{C e}(\mathrm{IV})$ cycles. As for $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2^{-}}$ MIL-88B/visible light, negligible TC degradation was achieved in the absence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , revealing that ROSs was yielded from the decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Meanwhile, without solid catalyst, TC was barely degraded indicating $\mathsf{F e}_{0.8}\mathsf{C e}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B was essential for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ activation. Consequently, TC could be efficiently degraded with the joint effect of $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and visible light.
Then, the generated ROSs responsible for TC degradation was analyzed. TBA, BQ and MeOH were used as the scavenger to elucidate the contribution of OH, $\cdot0_{2}^{-}$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ , respectively. As depicted in Fig. 8c, the addition of BQ and MeOH had slightly inhibitory influence on TC degradation whereas the addition of TBA almost completely inhibited TC degradation, indicating that OH was the dominant ROSs. On the other side, Mott-Schottky of $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2^{-}}$ MIL-88B was performed to help understand its photocatalytic activity in TC degradation (Fig. S6). The corresponding conduction band (CB) and valence band (VB) potentials of $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B were calculated as $-0.33\mathrm{eV}$ and $2.39\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. Apparently, OH cannot be formed in $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B/visible light system. Hence, the only way to generate OH is the heterogeneous Fenton process. Subsequently, the generation capacity of OH over MIL88B and $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}88\mathrm{B}$ in photo-Fenton process were measured by EPR spectra (Fig. 8d). Apparently, the typical fourfold peak belonged to DMPO- OH adduct was observed, which further identified the production of OH. More importantly, compared with MIL-88B, $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B exhibited the stronger intensity, suggesting production of more OH. It provides a direct proof for the higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ activation efficiency of $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B.
On the basis of the above analysis, the mechanism of TC degradation in the photo-Fenton process with $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B as catalysts was tentatively proposed, as demonstrated in Fig. 8e. Initially, the enlarged mesopores facilitated adsorption of more TC molecules inside or onto the surface of bimetallic MOFs. The TC-enriched microenvironment was formed in the vicinity of M CUSs, leading to the high accessibility between the TC molecules and OH. Upon addition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , more $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ molecules rapidly participated in the coordination benefiting from the abundant M CUSs. Subsequently, Fenton process was initiated between Fe(II)/Ce(III) and the adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Eqs. (1) and (2)), and the efficient dissociation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into OH occurred due to the lower activation energy barrier. Simultaneously, the $\mathrm{Fe(II)/Fe(III)}$ redox cycle could be continuously proceeded with the assistance of visible light (Eq. (3)), and the regeneration of Ce(III) was simply realized via the electron transfer from $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ to Ce(IV) (Eq. (4)), which is thermodynamically favorable [52]. The continuous activation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was insured under the multiple dynamic cycles of $\mathrm{Fe(II)/Fe(III)}$ and ${\mathsf{C e}}({\mathrm{III}})/{\mathsf{C e}}$ (IV), generating plenty of OH. Moreover, the fast mass-transfer of TC toward the pore channel enabled its rapid diffusion to the adsorption sites after the adsorption sites were released, which could efficiently utilize OH. Eventually, the TC was efficiently degraded by OH in photo-Fenton process.
$$
\begin{array}{l l}{{}}&{{\equiv\mathsf{F e}(\Pi)+\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}\to\equiv\mathsf{F e}(\Pi\mathsf{I})+\cdot\mathsf{O H}+\mathsf{O H}^{-}}}\\ {{}}&{{}}\\ {{}}&{{\equiv\mathsf{C e}(\Pi\mathsf{I})+\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}\to\equiv\mathsf{C e}(\mathrm{IV})+\cdot\mathsf{O H}+\mathsf{O H}^{-}}}\\ {{}}&{{}}\\ {{}}&{{\equiv\mathsf{F e}(\Pi\mathsf{I})-\mathsf{O}+\mathsf{h v}\to\equiv\mathsf{F e}(\Pi)-\mathsf{O}}}\\ {{}}&{{}}\\ {{}}&{{\equiv\mathsf{F e}(\Pi)+\mp\mathsf{C e}(\mathsf{I V})\to\equiv\mathsf{F e}(\Pi\mathsf{I})+\equiv\mathsf{C e}(\Pi\mathsf{I})}}\end{array}
$$
## 3.4. Influence of reaction parameters
The influence of reaction parameters (e.g., catalyst dosage, initial pH and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration) on TC degradation were examined in detail. As displayed in Fig. 9a and Fig. S7a, when dosage was below $0.10\ \mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , both the efficiency for TC degradation and the $k$ value increased with elevated dosage, and the TC could be nearly removed at $0.10\ \mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ within $15\ \mathrm{min}$ , the corresponding $k$ value achieved the maximum of $0.110\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . This originated from the explanation that the elevated catalyst dosages can provide more active sites available for the activation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Nevertheless, no further evident improvement for TC degradation as the dosage was above $0.10\ \mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . As a matter of fact, excessive catalysts will consume OH by the M CUSs (Eqs. (5) and (6)), and also affect light transmission due to aggregation of materials.
$$
\equiv{\mathrm{Fe}}({\mathrm{II}})+\bullet{\mathrm{OH}}\longrightarrow\in{\mathrm{Fe}}({\mathrm{III}})+{\mathrm{OH}}^{-}
$$
$$
\equiv C\Theta(\mathrm{III})+\mathrm{\bulletOH}\longrightarrow\equiv C\mathrm{e}(\mathrm{IV})+0\mathrm{H}^{-}
$$
As can be observed from Fig. 9b and Fig. S7b, there were strong TC degradation efficiency (being above $89.4~\%$ and the $k$ value (from 0.076 to $0.100\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ ) at initial pH range of 3–9. The good catalytic performance illustrated $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B could work on a wide applicable pH range as solid acid catalysts, which overcoming the drawback of the traditional Fenton system. But the degradation efficiency of TC sharply decreased to $65.8\ \%$ , and the $k$ value reduced to $0.030\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$ at $\mathsf{p H}\ 11$ . This may be ascribed to the reduced redox potential of OH under the alkaline $\mathrm{(E_{0}~=~}+2.8$ and $+2.0\mathrm{V}$ at $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{pH}}=0$ and 14, respectively) [41,53]. Also, the excessive $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ may compete with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ for M CUSs at high pH condition [26,54]. As observed in Fig. 9c, and Fig. S7c, the TC degradation was positively correlated with the concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in the range of $1{-}3\ \mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , whereas the overdose of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (above $3\ \mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ ) had inhibitive effects on the degradation of TC, and the optimal concentration was determined to be $3\ \mathrm{mL}\cdot\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . This is because excessive $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in turn acts as a scavenger for OH, as stated by Eqs. (7) and (8).
$$
\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}+^{\bullet}\mathrm{OH}\longrightarrow\mathrm{HO}_{2^{.}}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}
$$
$$
\mathrm{HO}_{2^{*}}+\mathbf{\bullet}0\mathrm{H}\rightarrow\mathrm{H}_{2}0+{\bf O}_{2}
$$
## 3.5. Reusability and stability
Four consecutive cycling experiments of TC degradation were performed to assess the reusability of $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B under identical conditions. The removal efficiency of TC and the corresponding TOC only had a slight decrease within four cycles (Fig. 9d). Accordingly, $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B exhibited excellent recyclability in the photo-Fenton process. Besides, the concentration of dissolved iron after each cycle was below the European Union environmental standard of $2\ \mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ (Fig. 9d), validating $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2^{-}}$ MIL-88B showed reliable structural stability. To sum it up, $\mathsf{F e}_{0.8^{-}}$ $\mathsf{C e}_{0.2}$ -MIL-88B having relatively good reusability and stability, is promising for environmental remediation.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we proposed the Ce doping strategy to promote the conversion of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in the Fenton-like system by regulating
Lewis acidity and local electron density of Fe-MOFs. The incorporation of Ce resulted in the ligand missing defects and the asymmetric electronic distribution in MIL-88B(Fe). The ligand missing defects generated more M CUSs, providing more active sites for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ coordination. The highly concentrated electrons density at Fe CUSs afforded the substantial electron donation, resulting in the low activation barrier towards robust $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ dissociation. Additionally, the adsorption enrichment and rapid mass-transfer of TC increased the collision possibility with OH. Moreover, the multiple dynamic cycles of $\mathrm{Fe(II)/Fe(III)}$ and $\mathsf{C e}(\mathrm{III})/\mathsf{C e}(\mathrm{IV})$ could continue to proceed duo to reduction of photo-generated electrons and the difference in standard redox potential. Furthermore, $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}–\mathrm{MIL}-$ 88B was applicable to wide applicable pH range, and could maintain good reusability and stability. The above results confirmed that $\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Ce}_{0.2}\mathrm{-MIL-}88\mathrm{B}$ can be a highly-efficient heterogeneous photo-Fenton catalysts for excellent environmental remediation. This work will inspire the further development of promising catalysts for the efficient heterogeneous photo-Fenton process based on the regulation of Lewis acidity and local electron density of active sites.
